WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:04.580
Crowds: Good morning John; it's Friday!

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.529
Hank: That, my brother, was a good morning
from a few of the shows that Hank Green and

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:09.940
the Perfect Strangers played together at our
last tour on the West Coast.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.100
I had them intro today's video because: one,
we're going on tour again on the East Coast

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.519
and two, because today I want to talk about
crowds.

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:17.880
Every social animal, it seems, gets a collective
noun.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.010
A school of fish, a gaggle of geese, a pod
of whales, a herd of cows.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:25.350
And there are lots of lesser know and super
awesome ones, like a business of ferrets or

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.980
a murder of crows or a crash of rhinoceros.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.510
Also a romp of otters; that's really a thing.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:35.260
But humans, since we mostly talk about ourselves,
have given ourselves a bunch of different

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.350
collective nouns, depending on how large the
group is, and what the group is doing.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.290
If we're working together, it's a crew; if
we're praying together, it's a congregation;

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:45.600
if you're fighting together, it's an army;
if you're mad and angry together, that's a

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.600
mob.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.400
But the most general term for a group of humans,
I think, is a crowd.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.580
Now since the communication revolution of
the internet, our society has become a little

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.360
bit obsessed with a new kind of crowd, I think.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.440
It's a crowd not tied together by being in
the same place but by common values or goals

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:01.440
or interests.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.400
The internet has enabled crowd funding like
Patreon and Indiegogo and Kickstarter, and

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.470
crowd-sourcing, which has built the marvel
that is Wikipedia.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:13.850
And then you got the wisdom of the crowd,
and the crowd is up voting things on Reddit

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.970
and deciding whether or not they want to watch
YouTube videos or share things on Facebook,

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:20.470
and that's helping all of those different
organizations figure out how to curate content

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:21.970
more efficiently and effectively.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:27.250
We can now, for the first time in human history,
literally say that we are alone in a crowd.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:32.470
And I think that we are forgetting that this
crowd is made up of individual people, and

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:33.840
I'm a little bit uncomfortable with that.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.410
But before I talk about why that is, I want
to say thank you to all of our Patreon patrons

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.960
who have helped to fund SciShow and Crash
Course, very effectively so far.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.480
We're almost two-thirds of the way to our
pre-Patreon switch funding levels, so thank

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:46.540
you so much for doing that.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:50.660
Now when we see that CrashCourse is raising
over $18,000 a month to fund educational,

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:55.320
free video on the internet, we have a hard
time, in our brains, thinking of that as being

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.000
done by individual people so we attribute
it to "the crowd."

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.010
But it's not the crowd; it's not the culture
-- it's individuals.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:07.820
It's a few people, really less than a percentage
of the total viewership of those shows, who

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:13.980
have that magical combination of ability and
enthusiasm that makes them interested in giving

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.230
us money even though they don't have to.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.691
The way that I understand "the crowd" and
of course, many people would call, like, the

00:02:18.691 --> 00:02:22.540
audience of this, the 200,000-some people
who are going to watch this video "the crowd."

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:26.459
The way that I understand it is heavily influenced
by actually going to places and seeing real

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:28.290
people, which is why I keep playing concerts.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:33.140
Up on a stage, you get a different, and much
more literal view of what a crowd is.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:37.780
It's a bunch of people, who have all individually
come together because they share a common

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:38.780
interest.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.099
From the stage I can see the crowd, both as
that marvellous human herd, all jumping in

00:02:43.099 --> 00:02:47.770
unison and also as a bunch of individual people,
making individual decisions.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:50.380
But when I'm in the audience, I feel it very
differently.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.750
Whether I'm in the audience that's participating
in a crowd funding campaign or I'm watching

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:59.230
music and I'm the one jumping up and down
in unison, knowing that my individual actions

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:04.030
are valuable, but they're only really meaningful
in combination with a dozen, or a hundred,

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.670
or a thousand, or a million other people.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.140
I love being my individual self.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.330
But there's also something really wonderful
about sacrificing that individuality for a

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.430
moment of giving or for a few hours of enjoyment
at a concert.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:17.340
I went to a lot of shows growing up -- a lot
of shows at grimy little venues, with a bunch

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.820
of people that I didn't know and that I would
never know, and those shows were scary.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.330
I found them scary pretty much every single
time I went to a show.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:27.270
But what I learned there wasn't that the crowd
was wise or powerful or effective, which is

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:28.300
what we talk about now.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.849
I think the thing I learned was actually much
more important than that.

00:03:30.849 --> 00:03:32.849
I learned that crowds are kind.

00:03:32.849 --> 00:03:34.720
Or at least they can be in the right situations.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.450
And I learned that if I let the barrier of
my individuality drop, I wasn't afraid of

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:40.450
what I saw.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:46.150
For a careful moment, I could stop being me,
and I could just be the crowd, and I would

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.530
gain a lot more than I would lose.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:53.610
John I'll see you on Friday, and nerdfighters
in these areas, I'll see you in April, I hope.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:58.250
[music] Can you even imagine way back in the
past when we thought that storms were caused

00:03:58.250 --> 00:03:59.450
by petty gods

